# Every setting this project has, in one file.
#
# The worker reads this file directly: worker/env.py finds the nearest .env walking
# up from the module, which is the repository root.
#
# The web app does not read it in production — Vercel holds its values, and the
# table below says which ones it needs there. `next dev` looks for .env in
# apps/web/, so a local dev server needs a copy or a symlink; nothing else does.
#
#   worker only   TG_*, TELEGRAM_OPS_CHAT, TELEGRAM_OPS_TOKEN
#   web only      + WA_VERIFY_TOKEN, WA_APP_SECRET
#   both          + WA_PHONE_NUMBER_ID, WA_ACCESS_TOKEN
#   web only      TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET, TELEGRAM_ADMIN_CHAT, ADMIN_*, STRIPE_*
#   both          DATABASE_URL, TELEGRAM_TOKEN, TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME, SITE_URL

# ── the database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The worker and the web want DIFFERENT ports on the same database, and this is
# not a detail to normalise away:
#
#   worker  session pooler, port 5432. It takes a session-scoped advisory lock to
#           stop two runs overlapping, and a transaction pooler cannot hold one —
#           the lock would report success and guard nothing.
#   web     transaction pooler, port 6543. No session locks, many short-lived
#           connections, which is what that pooler is for.
#
# Never the direct host (db.<ref>.supabase.co): it publishes an IPv6 address and no
# IPv4 one, so it resolves where IPv6 works and fails outright where it does not —
# with nothing changed on our side.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# ── Telegram: our bot ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From @BotFather. The worker sends alerts with it and the web answers commands
# with it, so it goes in both places.
TELEGRAM_TOKEN=
# Without the @. Builds the t.me links: the form's "Connect Telegram" button and
# the upgrade button on a free-tier alert.
TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=
# Any long random string. Passed to setWebhook and checked on every update, so that
# only Telegram can post to the webhook route.
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Where breakage is reported: a chat id, in practice your own chat with the bot.
# Find it by messaging @userinfobot. The bot cannot write to you until you have
# pressed Start on it — Telegram answers 403 until then, and the alert is lost.
TELEGRAM_OPS_CHAT=
# The bot that carries those alerts. Optional: left empty, TELEGRAM_TOKEN is used
# and operational warnings arrive in the same conversation as customer alerts.
# A separate bot keeps them apart, and it can be polled with getUpdates to read
# a chat id — which the customer bot cannot, because its webhook owns updates.
# Only the token matters; whose account created the bot in @BotFather does not.
TELEGRAM_OPS_TOKEN=
# The one chat allowed to run admin commands in the bot. Your own chat id.
TELEGRAM_ADMIN_CHAT=

# ── Telegram: reading the feed ────────────────────────────────────────────
# From https://my.telegram.org -> API development tools. These identify the
# application, not the account.
TG_API_ID=
TG_API_HASH=
# A live login to a PERSONAL account, printed once by `python -m worker login`.
# Treat it like a password. A bot session will not do: Telegram forbids bots from
# reading history, which is what this is for.
TG_SESSION=
# The chat to read, without the @.
TG_WATCH=
# Names this reader. The ingest cursor and the per-reader uniqueness are keyed by
# it, so two accounts must not share a name — and reusing a name across accounts
# makes the reader skip everything, silently.
TG_READER=
# Words replaced with "hs" in stored message bodies, comma separated. The source
# signs every message with its own brand, and that name has no business sitting in
# our database, our backups, or any report drawn from them.
TG_REDACT=

# ── the site ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where this app is reachable, no trailing slash. Used in every link the bot sends
# and in the upgrade page's URLs, so the worker needs it too.
SITE_URL=

# ── the admin console ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate both with:  node scripts/admin-hash.mjs '<your password>'
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH is a scrypt hash, not the password — the password itself is
# stored nowhere, including here. Without it the console has no way in, which is
# the safe direction for a missing secret to fail in.
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
# Signs the session cookie AND salts the visitor and login-attempt hashes. At least
# 32 characters. Changing it signs everybody out and makes yesterday's visitor
# hashes unrelated to today's, which is harmless.
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=

# ── Stripe ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Card payments. Inert unless STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is set: the deployment runs
# perfectly well with no way to pay, which is the state it starts in.
#
# There is deliberately no price here. A price belongs to a plan, so it lives in
# plans.stripe_price_id — adding a plan is then a row in the database rather than a
# deploy, and a plan whose row says thirty days can no longer be charged for seven.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# From the webhook's own page in the Stripe dashboard, not the API keys page.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ── WhatsApp ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Messages come from a WhatsApp Business account through Meta's Cloud API, so the
# sender is a business name rather than somebody's phone number.
#
# Two ways a listing can be sent, and the difference is not cosmetic:
#
#   * within 24 hours of the person's last message, free-form text with a link
#     preview — the same message Telegram gets, photo included, and free;
#   * outside it, only an approved template. Fixed shape, no photo, and Meta
#     charges per message.
#
# `user_channels.last_inbound_at` is what decides which, per recipient.
#
# WA_TEMPLATE_NAME is the template approved in the WhatsApp Manager; see the
# instructions for the exact body to paste. Leave it empty and listings outside
# the window stay queued rather than being sent as something Meta would refuse.
#
# WA_LINK_PREFIX is where the template's button points. A template button is a
# fixed prefix plus one variable, so the three portals cannot be linked directly:
# /l/<id> on our own domain redirects, and counts the click.
WA_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WA_ACCESS_TOKEN=
WA_TEMPLATE_NAME=new_listing
WA_TEMPLATE_LANGUAGE=en
WA_LINK_PREFIX=https://londonhomefinder.co.uk/l

# Whether the approved template declares a header of type IMAGE.
#
# WhatsApp makes its own thumbnail for a link preview — small, heavily
# compressed, no setting to change it — and a template carries no preview at
# all. So a listing with a picture is sent as a real image instead: inside the
# 24-hour window as an image message with the text as its caption, outside it as
# the template's image header.
#
# Leave this empty until the template has been re-approved WITH that header.
# Sending a component a template does not declare is error 132000, and every
# alert would fail.
WA_TEMPLATE_IMAGE=

# Web only. WA_VERIFY_TOKEN is any string you choose, given to Meta when the
# webhook is registered and echoed back once to prove the endpoint is yours.
# WA_APP_SECRET signs every delivery: without checking it, anyone who learns the
# url can claim to be any number.
WA_VERIFY_TOKEN=
WA_APP_SECRET=

# The number the site's "Connect to WhatsApp" button opens, digits only, country
# code first, no plus. Web only.
#
# Leave WA_PHONE_NUMBER_ID empty and the button does not appear at all, which is
# the state to be in until a business is registered: Meta's test number reaches
# five recipients you add by hand, and nobody else.
#
# The two messengers are unconnected. Signing up on both makes two accounts with
# two plans, and the free trial applies to each — recognising that it is one
# person would mean asking for a phone number or keeping a cookie, and neither is
# a fair price for policing two days.
WHATSAPP_NUMBER=
